  "s4": {
   "rows": [
    {
     "text": "                               Main nano help text                              ",
     "attrs": [
      {
       "start": 0,
       "end": 80,
       "attr": "reverse=True"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "                                                                                ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " The nano editor is designed to emulate the functionality and ease-of-use       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " of the UW Pico text editor.  There are four main sections of the editor.       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " The top line shows the program version, the current filename being             ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " edited, and whether or not the file has been modified.  Next is the main       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " editor window showing the file being edited.  The status line is the           ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " third line from the bottom and shows important messages.  The bottom two       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " lines show the most commonly used shortcuts in the editor.                     ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "                                                                                ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " Shortcuts are written as follows: Control-key sequences are notated with       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " a '^' and can be entered either by using the Ctrl key or pressing the Esc      ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " key twice.  Meta-key sequences are notated with 'M-' and can be entered        ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " using either the Alt, Cmd, or Esc key, depending on your keyboard setup.       ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " Also, pressing Esc twice and then typing a three-digit decimal number          ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " from 000 to 255 will enter the character with the corresponding value.         ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " The following keystrokes are available in the main editor window.              ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": " Alternative keys are shown in parentheses:                                     ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "                                                                                ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "^G     (F1)      Display this help text                                         ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "^X     (F2)      Close the current buffer / Exit from nano                      ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "                                                                                ",
     "attrs": []
    },
    {
     "text": "^L Refresh   ^F Where Is  M-B Previous ^P Prev Line ^Y Prev Page M-\\ First Line ",
